    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 79.66 efficiency score. The matchup against Abbott presents a prime opportunity for **Corbin Carroll** to leverage his speed, bolstered by a significant 19% SP skill advantage. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** is a high-confidence start at 72.92; his positive platoon split against Waguespack and his favorable spot in the three-hole make him our most reliable anchor for run production. While **Gabriel Moreno** benefits from the same favorable matchup against Abbott, **Brice Turang** also earns a strong recommendation (63.57), as his placement at the top of the lineup remains our most efficient engine for stolen base opportunities.\n\nRegarding our tougher calls, **Mickey Moniak** (54.67) and **Max Muncy** (59.01) land in the marginal tier, but they provide the best balance given the overall slate. **Isaac Paredes** was the most difficult exclusion from the starting nine; his 43.29 score fell just short of the threshold, primarily because he faces a tougher matchup against Basso compared to the upside offered by our infield corps. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor the status of **James Wood** and **Trevor Story**, who remain sidelined on the IL, limiting our depth as we chase categories. \n\nRisk management remains paramount today, particularly with **Austin Riley** (48.23) and **Griffin Conine** (44.43), both of whom are hindered by unfavorable platoon situations against Drohan and Kent, respectively. While **Seiya Suzuki** (50.23) is battling a difficult matchup against Miller, we are keeping him in the lineup as his elite sprint speed gives him a secondary path to value\u2014even if he struggles to put the ball in play. We have no major weather concerns currently, but given that some lineups are still pending, any late-scratch adjustments in order could shift the daily efficiency projections for **Jose Caballero** or **Josh Naylor**.",
